  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA slightly southwesterly flow aloft is over MT, as an upper level\ndisturbance is moving northeast from Glacier Park up into southern\nCanada. Expect light snow this morning along the divide to become\nmore scattered later today. Some partial clearing overnight has\nallowed dense fog to develop over portions of North Central MT\nearly this morning.\n\nFor Friday into Saturday, weak upper level ridging is forecast to\ndevelop over the CWA, along with seasonable temperatures.\n\nFor Saturday night through Sunday night...an upper level low over\nthe Pacific Northwest will move southeast towards Utah. This will\nresult in a moist southerly flow aloft to develop over Central MT.\nAs a result, rain/snow is expected to develop Saturday night over\nSouthwest MT and then move into Central MT by Sunday morning.\n\nThe precipitation will exit the area on Monday, as the next upper\nlevel ridge starts to build over the northern Rockies. The main \nquestion with this next upper level ridge is how strong will it \nbe? Overall, do expect drier and slight warmer conditions by the \nlater portions of next week as this upper level ridge develops.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe winter weather advisory for the Rocky Mountain will likely be\ncancelled by 6 AM, as snow is expected to lighter and begin to\ndiminish between 6 AM and Noon in that region.\n\nThe dense fog advisory will continue through this morning. There\nis the potential the dense fog advisory could be extended, as the\nfog will be slow to burn off.\n\nThe storm system this weekend will bring rain/snow to much of the\nCWA. Snow levels will be high to start, generally above 7000 feet\non Saturday, but then are forecast to fall down to about 4000 \nfeet by Monday. By Monday, most of the precipitation will have \nexited the region, but mountain snowfall is likely Saturday night \ninto Sunday. Snow accumulations around the Pony/Big Sky area are \nstarting to approach warning criteria. Thus a winter storm watch \nmight be needed for Zone 330. Otherwise, a winter weather advisory\nis looking likely for mountain areas of Southwest and portions of\nCentral MT from Saturday night into Sunday night.\n\nLooking ahead to the middle of next week, if the upper level ridge\ndevelops and is a bit stronger than currently forecast, afternoon\ntemperatures might be a bit warmer than currently forecast for the\nlater half of next week. Brusda",
